WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:02.801
-Our next guest is
an award-winning cartoonist,

00:00:02.801 --> 00:00:04.434
author, and illustrator.
The latest volume of his

00:00:04.434 --> 00:00:06.100
best-selling
graphic-novel series,

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:09.667
"Hilo Book 9: Gina
and the Last City on Earth,"

00:00:09.667 --> 00:00:11.701
is available now.
Please welcome back to the show

00:00:11.701 --> 00:00:13.734
our friend Judd Winick,
everybody!

00:00:13.734 --> 00:00:16.000
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:22.400
♪♪

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:28.801
♪♪

00:00:28.801 --> 00:00:30.567
Welcome back.
-It's good to be here.

00:00:30.567 --> 00:00:32.901
-I can't remember which of these
you were here for

00:00:32.901 --> 00:00:34.701
the first time, but this is
now the ninth of these.

00:00:34.701 --> 00:00:36.234
-Yes.
-Very popular with the kids.

00:00:36.234 --> 00:00:38.467
And you've actually been
going out and visiting schools

00:00:38.467 --> 00:00:40.434
to promote the book.
Are those fun visits?

00:00:40.434 --> 00:00:42.000
-They are.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:45.501
It's been -- It's been a month
on the road. About 50 schools.

00:00:45.501 --> 00:00:47.934
I do school assemblies.
I kill it.

00:00:47.934 --> 00:00:49.033
I'm really good at assemblies.

00:00:49.033 --> 00:00:50.434
-You're really --
The kids like it?

00:00:50.434 --> 00:00:52.267
I will say --
'Cause at a certain age,

00:00:52.267 --> 00:00:54.367
it's very nerve-racking
being in front of young kids

00:00:54.367 --> 00:00:56.467
in an assembly. They're not
used to having a good time.

00:00:56.467 --> 00:00:58.267
-No, I'm -- [ Scoffs ]

00:00:58.267 --> 00:00:59.968
It's, you know --
I play the big rooms.

00:00:59.968 --> 00:01:01.400
I'm good at it.

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:05.067
Your cafeterias, your
auditoriums, your cafetoriums.

00:01:05.067 --> 00:01:07.067
I'm very --
I mean, mind you --

00:01:07.067 --> 00:01:08.801
Mind you,
it's a school assembly.

00:01:08.801 --> 00:01:10.767
They're out of class
for about an hour.

00:01:10.767 --> 00:01:12.367
-That helps. Yeah.
-Yeah, their level of happiness

00:01:12.367 --> 00:01:14.367
is about an 8 out of 10 anyway.
-Right.

00:01:14.367 --> 00:01:15.801
-But, you know, I'm not --

00:01:15.801 --> 00:01:18.200
I'm not there talking
about fire safety

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:20.767
or from the Dairy Council
about how milk is made.

00:01:20.767 --> 00:01:23.300
-Yeah. I know you. You care
not at all about fire safety.

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:24.934
-No. No.
-Now, what about --

00:01:24.934 --> 00:01:26.901
Do you ever get --
That's a fun age

00:01:26.901 --> 00:01:28.701
for questions
from the audience.

00:01:28.701 --> 00:01:31.434
Does anybody ask you
anything memorable?

00:01:31.434 --> 00:01:34.100
-No. I mean -- Well, it's gone
wrong a couple of times.

00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:36.434
I mean, I --
So, I'm a cartoonist.

00:01:36.434 --> 00:01:38.767
I draw. So part
of my presentation

00:01:38.767 --> 00:01:40.200
is, I'll draw pictures,
I'll take requests.

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:41.934
Kids ask, "Will you draw Hilo?"

00:01:41.934 --> 00:01:43.367
"Yeah, I'll draw Hilo."

00:01:43.367 --> 00:01:45.300
"Will you draw
Gina fighting a dinosaur?"

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:47.834
"Sure."
And a couple of years back,

00:01:47.834 --> 00:01:50.701
a little girl, second grader,
raised her hand and said,

00:01:50.701 --> 00:01:52.067
"Will you draw a penis?"

00:01:52.067 --> 00:01:54.000
[ Laughter ]

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:57.234
And they -- All 300 kids
didn't so much laugh,

00:01:57.234 --> 00:02:00.434
as they just started
screaming simultaneously.

00:02:00.434 --> 00:02:02.801
I mean, like the Beatles
came over from Liverpool

00:02:02.801 --> 00:02:04.467
kind of screaming.

00:02:04.467 --> 00:02:06.200
-Yeah, that would be
hard to process.

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:08.267
If somebody had the nerve
to do that in second grade,

00:02:08.267 --> 00:02:10.100
I think I --
It would blow my mind.

00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:12.267
-Oh. I couldn't get 'em back.

00:02:12.267 --> 00:02:13.701
It was -- It just --
It was over with.

00:02:13.701 --> 00:02:15.300
I mean, they're having
this instantaneous --

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:18.667
-They just start going,
"Draw it! Draw it!"

00:02:18.667 --> 00:02:20.534
-Yeah. No.

00:02:20.534 --> 00:02:23.501
-You have a, you know,
crazy story

00:02:23.501 --> 00:02:24.767
about how you met your wife.

00:02:24.767 --> 00:02:26.801
I mean, we all have
good meeting stories,

00:02:26.801 --> 00:02:30.200
but you met your wife
on a season of "The Real World."

00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:32.400
-Yeah.
-1994. San Francisco.

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:33.567
Here you are...

00:02:33.567 --> 00:02:34.934
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:34.934 --> 00:02:37.534
...with your wife, Pam.

00:02:37.534 --> 00:02:40.000
And, I mean, at the time,
you know,

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:41.901
she was a doctor.
-Yeah.

00:02:41.901 --> 00:02:43.868
I mean, you know,
I'm a cartoonist.

00:02:43.868 --> 00:02:45.901
She's a doctor.
Makes for an interesting combo.

00:02:45.901 --> 00:02:47.901
-Basically if you don't meet
on a reality show,

00:02:47.901 --> 00:02:49.400
you never meet.

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:52.701
-No. I mean, you know, Pam --
Pam is magna cum laude.

00:02:52.701 --> 00:02:56.200
Phi Beta Kappa from Harvard.
Okay. And a doctor.

00:02:56.200 --> 00:02:58.501
Me, I, you know -- I make things
up and draw them, right?

00:02:58.501 --> 00:03:00.467
So, you know...
-It's very nice, though,

00:03:00.467 --> 00:03:02.801
because I feel like
there are so many reality shows

00:03:02.801 --> 00:03:06.267
that are designed to make people
who are strangers fall in love.

00:03:06.267 --> 00:03:08.367
That's not actually
what "The Real World" was.

00:03:08.367 --> 00:03:10.300
And then that's what you guys
managed to pull off.

00:03:10.300 --> 00:03:11.601
-Oh, yeah.
I mean, it's helpful

00:03:11.601 --> 00:03:13.133
that we started dating
after the show.

00:03:13.133 --> 00:03:14.334
-Yeah, that's good.

00:03:14.334 --> 00:03:15.634
-Very helpful we started
dating after the show.

00:03:15.634 --> 00:03:16.801
-Yeah. Not on camera.
-No.

00:03:16.801 --> 00:03:18.033
And they were really pissed
about it, too.

00:03:18.033 --> 00:03:19.234
-[ Laughs ]
Oh, right, of course.

00:03:19.234 --> 00:03:20.534
-Oh, no. We heard later
from the producers.

00:03:20.534 --> 00:03:22.868
Like, "We had an episode,
like, a 747

00:03:22.868 --> 00:03:24.667
gassed up, ready to go.

00:03:24.667 --> 00:03:27.734
But not you guys! You just had
to wait till the cameras left!"

00:03:27.734 --> 00:03:29.100
Yeah, that was us.

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:30.601
-So, Hilo --
this wonderful character.

00:03:30.601 --> 00:03:33.634
Every time he sees someone,
he screams.

00:03:33.634 --> 00:03:35.934
We can sort of see up there.
-Yes.

00:03:35.934 --> 00:03:38.934
-He yells, you know, "Aaaah!"
-It's a running gag.

00:03:38.934 --> 00:03:40.133
It's been a running --

00:03:40.133 --> 00:03:41.634
for about nine --
for about nine years.

00:03:41.634 --> 00:03:43.000
-Nine books of this.
-Yes.

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:45.234
-And your fans noticed something
about you and Pam

00:03:45.234 --> 00:03:46.701
that you guys had not noticed.

00:03:46.701 --> 00:03:48.167
-Yeah, we got
two demographics now.

00:03:48.167 --> 00:03:50.033
So we've got, like, the parents
who saw us on the show

00:03:50.033 --> 00:03:52.100
and we got the kids.
The parents showed the kids,

00:03:52.100 --> 00:03:54.033
like, our Instagram feed,
and the kids will say,

00:03:54.033 --> 00:03:55.901
"You and Pam!
You do the Hilo greeting."

00:03:55.901 --> 00:03:57.334
It's like, no, we don't.
-There you go. That's...

00:03:57.334 --> 00:03:58.601
-And then we --

00:03:58.601 --> 00:04:01.567
-So, this is not --
And this, by the way,

00:04:01.567 --> 00:04:03.834
is something you've been doing
from early on.

00:04:03.834 --> 00:04:05.767
There's the wedding photo.
-That's our wedding picture.

00:04:05.767 --> 00:04:08.033
[ Laughter ]

00:04:08.033 --> 00:04:10.234
I don't -- I don't know
how it started.

00:04:10.234 --> 00:04:12.400
I think -- We met on reality TV.

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:15.100
And rule one is, like,
you never look at the camera.

00:04:15.100 --> 00:04:17.934
So now that we look at
the camera, it was like, "Yeah!"

00:04:17.934 --> 00:04:21.000
-You're out of practice.
-Yeah, no, it's stupid.

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:22.634
But, you know --
But we're happy.

00:04:22.634 --> 00:04:25.467
-You -- I've been lucky enough
to meet your kids.

00:04:25.467 --> 00:04:26.868
Your kids are teenagers.

00:04:26.868 --> 00:04:28.434
Last time you were here,
you said they loved the books.

00:04:28.434 --> 00:04:30.734
Have they outgrown them?
How are they doing with it?

00:04:30.734 --> 00:04:33.934
-They are teenagers now.
They are 17 and 14.

00:04:33.934 --> 00:04:36.701
And in the early days,
I mean, they were little

00:04:36.701 --> 00:04:38.634
when I started the book,
and it was always, you know,

00:04:38.634 --> 00:04:41.400
like, "Dad, it's so funny
what Hilo burps.

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:42.934
I like that part."

00:04:42.934 --> 00:04:44.834
Now they're older,
and they have notes.

00:04:44.834 --> 00:04:47.501
-Yeah.
-You know, it's like, you know,

00:04:47.501 --> 00:04:49.834
"I don't think this third act
is working out quite the way

00:04:49.834 --> 00:04:52.367
you wanted it to, you know?"
-Does it make you

00:04:52.367 --> 00:04:55.300
a little proud
that they have an opinion?

00:04:55.300 --> 00:04:56.734
-It's entirely my fault

00:04:56.734 --> 00:04:58.834
because they live in my house
and I ruin everything.

00:04:58.834 --> 00:05:00.634
-Yeah.
-Like, just watching

00:05:00.634 --> 00:05:02.300
a television show or a movie,

00:05:02.300 --> 00:05:04.067
it's like, "Okay, okay,
this is the second-act con--

00:05:04.067 --> 00:05:06.033
There's a second-act conflict
coming up right now, guys."

00:05:06.033 --> 00:05:07.300
-Oh, so they learned it
from you.

00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:08.801
-It's my fault.
-Yeah. Yeah.

00:05:08.801 --> 00:05:10.701
Well, it's very nice that
you've been able to look inward

00:05:10.701 --> 00:05:12.167
and make that observation
about yourself.

00:05:12.167 --> 00:05:13.901
-Yeah, I know who I am.

00:05:13.901 --> 00:05:15.868
-Congratulations on nine books
in the series.

00:05:15.868 --> 00:05:17.400
It is so lovely
to have you back.

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:20.000
You guys, one more time
for Judd Winick.

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:23.701
"Hilo Book 9: Gina
and the Last City on Earth"

00:05:23.701 --> 00:05:25.501
is available now.
And everyone in the audience --

00:05:25.501 --> 00:05:27.434
Everyone in the audience
is getting a copy.

00:05:27.434 --> 00:05:29.300
They're wonderful books.
We'll be right back

00:05:29.300 --> 00:05:30.968
with music from Ruston Kelly.

